# Security guidance

Do not write any of the following without an explicit, recorded human decision to
override it:

- Hardcoded secrets (API keys, passwords, tokens) in source.
- SQL built by string interpolation — use parameterized queries instead.
- Disabled certificate/host validation, or an auth token stored somewhere a script on
  the page can read it.
- A destructive migration (cascade delete, irreversible schema change) without an
  approved plan naming it explicitly.
- A newly exposed endpoint or handler with no auth/authorization check, unless the task
  explicitly documents it as intentionally public.

When reviewing existing code that already has one of these patterns, flag it — do not
silently rewrite it, and do not silently accept it either. This guidance is advisory;
the actual enforcement boundary is hooks and sandbox permissions, not this text.
